Control flow

Create an object x containing a single number. Write an if/else statement that prints "positive" if x is greater than zero, and "zero or negative" otherwise. Try it with a few different values of x.







NoteSolution






x &lt;- -3
if (x &gt; 0) {
  print("positive")
} else {
  print("zero or negative")
}

[1] "zero or negative"






Extend your answer to question 1 so that it prints "positive", "negative" or "zero".







TipHint



Use else if to add a second condition.








NoteSolution






x &lt;- 0
if (x &gt; 0) {
  print("positive")
} else if (x &lt; 0) {
  print("negative")
} else {
  print("zero")
}

[1] "zero"






Select the body mass (body_mass) of the first penguin in the penguins dataset. Use if/else to create an object called size that is "heavy" if the penguin weighs 4,000 grams or more, and "light" otherwise.







NoteSolution






first_mass &lt;- penguins$body_mass[1]
if (first_mass &gt;= 4000) {
  size &lt;- "heavy"
} else {
  size &lt;- "light"
}
size

[1] "light"






Now try to do the same for every penguin, by replacing penguins$body_mass[1] with penguins$body_mass. What happens, and why?







NoteSolution






if (penguins$body_mass &gt;= 4000) {
  size &lt;- "heavy"
} else {
  size &lt;- "light"
}

Error in `if (penguins$body_mass &gt;= 4000) ...`:
! the condition has length &gt; 1


if expects a condition that is a single TRUE or FALSE. Here, the condition penguins$body_mass &gt;= 4000 is a vector with one value per penguin, so R stops with an error.




Use ifelse() to create a new column in penguins called size, containing "heavy" or "light" for every penguin. Tabulate the new column with table().







NoteSolution






penguins$size &lt;- ifelse(penguins$body_mass &gt;= 4000, "heavy", "light")
table(penguins$size)


heavy light 
  177   165 






Two penguins have a missing body mass. What value do they get in your new size column? Check using table(penguins$size, useNA = "ifany").







NoteSolution






table(penguins$size, useNA = "ifany")


heavy light  &lt;NA&gt; 
  177   165     2 


They are NA. When the condition is NA, ifelse() can’t decide between "heavy" and "light", so it returns NA. This is usually what you want: it keeps missing values missing.




Use ifelse() with the moy vector you created above to label each month as "summer" (June, July and August) or "not summer".







TipHint



The %in% operator checks whether each element of one vector appears in another, e.g. moy %in% c("June", "July").








NoteSolution






ifelse(moy %in% c("June", "July", "August"), "summer", "not summer")

 [1] "not summer" "not summer" "not summer" "not summer" "not summer"
 [6] "summer"     "summer"     "summer"     "not summer" "not summer"
[11] "not summer" "not summer"
